The `res.redirect` method sets the `Location` header and the status code as follows:

- It defaults to a status code of `302` (Found) if no status is provided.
- If a status code is provided, it uses that value; otherwise, it defaults to `302`.
- It calls `this.location(address).get('Location')` to compute the final URL for the `Location` header, which ensures proper URL encoding and path resolution.
- The `Location` header is then set in the response headers.
- The status code is set directly on the response object, which is sent in the HTTP response.

This behavior is defined in `lib/response.js:801-867`:

```javascript
res.redirect = function redirect(url) {
  var address = url;
  var body;
  var status = 302;

  // allow status / url
  if (arguments.length === 2) {
    status = arguments[0]
    address = arguments[1]
  }

  if (!address) {
    deprecate('Provide a url argument');
  }

  if (typeof address !== 'string') {
    deprecate('Url must be a string');
  }

  if (typeof status !== 'number') {
    deprecate('Status must be a number');
  }

  // Set location header
  address = this.location(address).get('Location');

  // Support text/{plain,html} by default
  this.format({
    // ...
  });
};
```

The `this.location()` method is responsible for resolving the URL and encoding it properly (e.g., URL-encoding special characters), which is then used as the value of the `Location` header. The status code is set via the `status` variable and is applied to the response when the response is sent.

This is validated in test cases such as `test/res.redirect.js`, where assertions confirm that the `Location` header is set correctly and the status code is `302` by default.

